# SHA-256 of the official CPython embeddable archives this project builds on.
# scripts/build-portable.ps1 refuses to build from an archive whose hash is
# pinned here and does not match, and deletes the offending download.
#
# Adding a line: run the build once, check the SHA-256 it prints against the
# release page on python.org, then re-run with -PinHash. Pinning without
# checking just launders an unverified download into a trusted-looking file.
#   https://www.python.org/downloads/release/python-<version-without-dots>/
#
# Format: <version>-<arch>  <sha256>
#
# No entries yet -- the v1.4.1 package was built from an interpreter that was
# already unpacked on the build machine, so its original archive was not
# available to hash. The next `build-portable.ps1 -PinHash` run fills this in.

# 3.12.7 amd64, pinned 2026-09-21. Checked three ways before pinning:
#   1. MD5 4c0a5a44d4ca1d0bc76fe08ea8b76adc, as listed on the python.org
#      release page, matches the download.
#   2. python.org's Sigstore bundle for this file (python-3.12.7-embed-amd64.zip
#      .sigstore) states this SHA-256 as the signed digest, signer
#      thomas@python.org (the 3.12 release manager) via accounts.google.com;
#      the Rekor transparency-log entry 135667535 records the same hash. The
#      bundle's signature itself was not verified cryptographically (that needs
#      the sigstore tool).
#   3. All 31 .exe/.dll/.pyd inside carry valid Authenticode signatures, from
#      the Python Software Foundation (vcruntime140*.dll: Microsoft), checked
#      with Windows' own Get-AuthenticodeSignature.
3.12.7-amd64  0d57bb6cb078b74d23dbfe91f77d6780d45bed328911609f1f7ee2ba1606bf44
